commentBody:
@Cat Lady Margaret

I learned semordnipap, too!

Maybe some Corona beer for the regality?

@Steve C
I'm no astronomer, but it seems to me that a good way to detect... or show...faint meteors would be with a timelapse photo through a low- power TELESCOPE.

Yeah, I'm trying for charitable. It's a stretch.

@J.S.

Ned was 100% correct.  Inability to solve does not equal bad puzzle.

Sometimes it's best to be blunt.

@Guy

Ach.  Yes, cabernet, not cabaret.  

I have the same trouble with "calvary" and "cavalry".

@I

Fair enough! 

I suppose it's nice for Faraday to get a moment in the spotlight.

@Ned S
@Francis

I interpreted that one a little differently. HAIRY definitely comes from “tressed”, but it’s a HAIRY BACK only because each them answer has an element of reversal in it, as an extension of the theme. Which is why other answers include words like COUNTER and RETRO.

But that doesn’t take away the ick feeling I get from associating a hairy back with dessert.

@Guy
This is the spot that got me, and I had to look up the answer key.  I had tAB -- thinking you can run a tab anywhere at a bar.  But tOWED didn't make sense, and I wasn't getting any happy music.

Since I don't drink alcohol, so don't frequent bars, the CAB I assume is short for Cabernet, which is not high on the list of things I remember.....

@Francis

You know how you're supposed to feel a shimmer of satisfaction when you solve a late-week?

I felt complicit when I finished this one.

@Barry Ancona

Clues and fills have sounds, too, unc.

A good crossword is a synesthetic affair.

@SP

It wasn't too tricky.  Never said it was.  Filled the nonsense fairly easily.

It was too...cringe.

@Francis

And then, TIL that "nil" actually is used is Lisp, Scala, and Pascal.

Who knew?

@Jeff I have a vague memory of seeing "nil" in textbooks, and searching shows that some languages actually use it as a keyword. They tend to be what I'd call the more "academic" languages, as opposed to the languages that dominate the industry (with some exceptions).

But I thought it was NULL, so I tentatively filled in NUL. That's the spelling used for the ASCII "null character", which is not quite the same thing, but the only three-letter option that came to mind at first!

All that being said, as a computer programmer myself, I'm happy to see the entry clued as programming trivia rather than the usual football/soccer trivia. :-)

@Francis

Oh, I totally forgot about Lisp and Scala.

But also, as I’ve noted elsewhere: Go and Ruby. And I’m sure there are plenty of others!

@Jeff

Your comment is wrong. Two mainstream programming languages that use “nil”: Go and Ruby.

I’ll just never understand comments that contain a supremely confident pronouncement regarding a topic the commenter is clearly ignorant or confused about…

@Anonymous
I was guilty of such a pronouncement...twice actually.
Learned my lesson; now it's my chief reason for being entertained by this forum.

@Steve

Don't sweat it, bro.

It only means you are of sound mind.

@Laura

You have to read it as two words.

You can DAB AT something on your face with a hankie.

@Jane Wheelaghan

Hydrangea, hibiscus, hyacinth.  So easy to blurt out the wrong one, and I've grown all three.

And it's "fuchsia," not "fuschia."  A common mistake.  Most plants ending in -ia are named for someone: Fuchs, Zinn, and aptly, Garden.

@Nancy J.
You are a tough crowd (all by your own self!)
I did solve this puzzle (without completely understanding it) and I read some Comments that were helpful in clarifying the style of puzzle.  It was plenty difficult for a Thursday, I thought, especially because it's so unique.

@Mean Old Lady

It must have been a wavelength thing.  I see now that many people had trouble with it.

I *did* enjoy it and found it to be very well constructed and unique.

@Commenter to "clear" something is to "bus" it? I've never heard of this. Much like DAMs hold water and have little to do with lords or sires (NB: Anyone else think everything horse related has a bunch of made up terms to ostracize the plebeians? Much like anything ship or tennis related.), the plural of bass ought to be basses (like cellos!), etc.

It's hard when it's challenging. It's frustrating when the words are factoids, archaisms, names, and jargon.

I liked where the theme wanted to go, but it felt forced. Smart = chic, therefore add retro?

@miniMeissner

BASSI is not the plural of "bass."  It's the plural of "basso," the Italian and therefore technical name for a bass singer.

If you're involved enough to know Bass I and Bass II, I'd think you'd recognize the Italian musical terms and their plurals.

@Pat-a-pon
I agree with you on BUS. As far as I can work out, to bus is a strictly US term meaning to clear tables. I only know it from hearing the term in US films and tv shows.
The equine terms I do know, thanks to horse mad daughter. Years of hanging around stables while she rode taught me more than I ever wanted to know about them; not only am I allergic to their dander, they scare the crap out of me.

@Helen Wright

Made me think of the soundtrack of my high school days:

<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VkKxmnrRVHo" target="_blank">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VkKxmnrRVHo</a>

@Barry Ancona
Also 'sleeping in'. And
@Helen Wright
I missed your comment and several entire threads.  
Emu emperors, what gives? You may be the executive branch  but, around here, we're the legislature with the power of the purse  and (unlike your spineless Congress) we are prepared to use it.  Loosen up. 🤨

@Helen Wright

Some people are commenting that "sleep in" is equivalent, but sleeping in is actually sleeping, not being awake but choosing to stay in bed for awhile anyway. I thought "lie in" meant that you're awake and choosing to stay in bed, but am I wrong about that? Now I'm very curious to know.

If it does mean that, the equivalent would be "stayed in bed", basically. Like, "I stayed in bed until 10 this last Saturday and it was glorious". If you were asleep that whole time, the only change would be from "stayed in bed" to "slept in".

@Helen Wright

Regarding your off topic--I, too, have offered bribes to the emus for some help in understanding them.

They maintain a god-like silence.

@SBK in TO
For your first meaning, I personally wouldn't ever use "I slept in!" I would instead say "I overslept!"
I guess in my family growing up it was only ever used with forethought. You make a plan to sleep in.

@Steve L
Harpers magazine runs one (I think similar) every month.
I've been tearing them out and saving them for years but haven't gotten around to figuring thrm out. Totally opaque.

@jak
Oh, it comes up almost daily for us quilters!

Ha ha ha ha

@D Bean
Neo--played by Keanu Reeves--is a character in The Matrix trilogy of films; near the beginning of the first, he is offered a choice between two pills--one blue, one red.
(Spoiler alert--they're not really pills.)

@jak and @Rich in ATL

I thought EN=N for NON  and BY=Bi for BINARY was the better reading.
But either way, we got there.

@Brian

First of all, it's not just a "she".  It was a joint construction.

And second, please don't wish ill on the constructor(s).

@Michael

If you'd like to understand the theme, Jim Horne spells it out in excruciating detail:

<a href="https://www.xwordinfo.com/Crossword?date=10/8/2026" target="_blank">https://www.xwordinfo.com/Crossword?date=10/8/2026</a>

@jennie
I too was happy to see Michael Faraday get a shout out!
Also happy to learn a new word for me "tisanes" but can someone explain NEO as red pilled to me?

@George K.

I'm going to assume you're speaking to the generic brand of Francis, the one in the movie "Stripes".  Not just me in particular.  

Although, to be fair, lightening up wouldn't be the worst advice I've ever gotten.

@J 

"Meteor shower" was fairly obvious to me once I tried interpreting "shower" as " something that performs the act of showing."  I had no idea at sll what the italicized clues were all about, though.

Who would use a telescope to look at meteors, though? It seems like it woul be really difficult to keep it in view as it shot across the sky, and even if you had some computerized tracking device to control the telescope so that it tracked the meteor, how much more detail are you going to get of the thing as opposed to looking at it with the naked eye? Most of them are pretty small and are only visible because they're glowing.

@Nancy J.

It’s like a bad joke. You can explain it as many times as you want, but if it’s not funny it’s just not funny.

That doesn’t mean everyone will think the joke wasn’t funny, but when a large portion of people feel a certain way, perhaps the problem is not the people.

@SP

To extend my analogy, I doubt that people who think a joke was bad would take the time to really think about why that was, but probably wouldn’t care because once the joke is told, it’s over.

With a puzzle, there’s a lot of time and energy spent trying to figure out a theme that was a bad joke, if that makes sense. There’s no desire to engage with it, there’s feelings of wasted time, and the idea that they are the butt of the bad joke. Just some thoughts.

@Nancy J.

💯. But there are lots of people who come to this forum to vent after having a bad solving experience, and such commenters often express their frustration at having been stumped by a puzzle as criticism of the puzzle’s quality.

One thing such commenters simply don’t seem able to see: as much as they may insist that they are NOT complaining just because a puzzle was too hard for them, the tone of their comments (angry/frustrated) gives them away.

@Nan C.

The word(s) you get by reading the clues, backwards are the actual clues.

The thing is that some of the answers are quite a stretch, but you can see how they're connected to the clues.

@Steve L

Thanks for the suggestion. I came back to the comments because I felt I didn't quite get the trick, and yes, Jim Horne's explanation filled the gap.

But even so, it all feels a little cumbersome. Not one of my favorite puzzles, to tell the truth.

@Captain Quahog

Well if clues are simply pointers that suggest an answer without being a strict definition or 100% accurate, then telescope is a fine answer.

But I get it, most of us have our pet peeves, so to speak.

@Barry Ancona

I interpreted the statement that the puzzle was good despite the fact that there was a clue that suggested a telescope could be used to look for meteorites as a criticism of the clue.

But I see now that I might have been wrong.

@Anna in Bellingham
It's explained, again and again, in the column and in the many comments that appeared before yours.
I mean ( not just you) do you want to get it, or just to complain that you didn't?

@Anna in Bellingham: you’re really close to getting it!
Because “smart” is “chic” as you noted, the actual clue “trams” is “retro-chic” - I.e. backwards.
Same for the others.

@Anna in Bellingham
I did not see your comment as a complaint but as a request for explanation.

Unfortunately, due to other complaints and dissing the constructors, I think some people were already fed up by the time you posted.

@Allison from Philly

"Others dream things that never were and ask why not."

George Bernard Shaw

@Allison from Philly

Why?  Because it is Thursday.

You realize for a large number of solvers the theme made complete sense.  Aren't you curious why?

@SP

Most marvelously a Milwaukee mind meld! 

🖖

@HeathieJ

Admit it--how much you love Star Trek follows closely on how much you love Patrick Stewart.

I can't say as I blame you.  He's one of the most compelling actors I've ever seen.  How they got him for Star Trek I'll never understand.  Big Shakespearean voice and temperament...just what's needed for the King of the Enterprise.

@Monte
AI first told me it was a unit of capacitance, but then I saw it was confusing the Faraday constant with the faraday unit and actually had both incorrect.

1 faraday = 9.648 x 10^4 C

@Charles Peterson

OK. Fine.  You have an opinion on their opinions.
So what?

@Jim
There is an Ontario in California.
No, really.

@HeathieJ - And today's comments are chock full of wildly incorrect but confidently stated nits, and many of them are "liked" as top comments too -- thereby demonstrating something I've been quite sure of for months now - a lot of the new commenters just aren't very well informed, which might explain why they find any but the easiest of puzzles unsolvable. 

The most egregious example: claiming that the FARADY is a unit of capacitance. 

But HERB TEA not "being a thing" is right up there.

@SBK in TO - But I am commenting upon people who are claiming, incorrectly, that the two are the same. I am not commenting out of the blue -- it is not I who brought jp the subject you think is too esoteric.

Are you saying that it is out of bounds to point out when someone states, with great certainty, something that is factually 100% incorrect? Just because some people think the topic is esoteric? 

Even when it is something that is soooooo easily checked in under 5 seconds with a simple search of the googles?

@Barry Ancona

Nope, AROUND by itself doesn't improve things imo. It's an adverb which requires an action such as "turn," "rotate" or "wheel" to be a reversal. TURNAROUNDDRINKS would fit the gimmick but that's obviously no good. It's just not as tidy as the others.

BTW it's OF A, not ON A.

@Steve- They are not called meteors UNTIL they enter the atmosphere and are "burned up."

So, no, I have not seen meteors before they "burn up" in the atmosphere, since such things do not exist.  

FYI, METEORITE is the remains of a meteor once it has landed here on the planet Earth.

@RobJ
A WORD. I pick out a WORD from boring meetings. 
Sigh.

@Lynn

I guess I mean I only had one 'aha' moment where I confidently filled in the rest of the letters. Certainly didn't get any without any crossing letters at all.

The other three I was able to put in the last one or two letters to make English words, but my reaction was much closer to "really? I guess..." than it was to "aha"

@Ron
So funny. AI actually gave me contradictory answers, so I went to Wikipedia.

It's been a long time since I studied E&M.

@Lynn

Ok.  This puzzle took me an hour and three minutes.

Slow solvers, unite!  We have nothing to fear but fear itself.  Or something.

@Jim
Yeah, that's one thing I like about xwstats. It gives me the percentage my time varies from my own recent solves on that day of the week. 

It also gives the average time posted for that puzzle for that day and I can only roll my eyes. I can't even type as fast as those folks solve. But it also says things like, "24% of solvers solved it faster than their Thursday average," which does provide a comparison. I'd trust those comparisons more if a wider swath of users posted.

@Lynn

Oooh, lentils in it! Love it! Chili or kidney beans are not for me. I'm a small red bean person, but lentils sounds amazing! 

I do like spicy, but if you ever make it for me, I'll bring my own hot sauce to add, no worries! There's always some in my purse!! I always use ground turkey too! 😊

@Kyle
The theme clues were in italics.

I used to see the deity translated as Shiva, but more recently have almost always seen it Siva.

@Niall

We say we "sleep in".  

"Weekends I sleep in till someone forces me out of bed."

@Chris Adams


I think you missed half the word play? 

ARGUMENT is a synonym for "spat" and it's COUNTER because the clue is really "taps" and needs to be reversed for it to make sense. Same thing with all the other clues... if you're "tressed" you are technically HAIRY and the BACK comes from the required reversal of "desserts" etc etc etc.
